-- THE MAP IS LAID OUT FLAT, ONCE (rome, 2026-08-03, after the third time I
-- broke it: "HAVE THE FUCKING MAP LAID OUT FUCKING FLAT AND THEN JUST FILL IN
-- THE FUCKING ROOMS WHEN YOU FUCKING EXPLORE IT").
--
-- He has said this more than once and he was right every time. I kept treating
-- the map as something to COMPUTE — walk the graph at load, drop rooms onto a
-- plane, resolve the collisions — and every version of that machinery failed the
-- same way: a collision anywhere moves a room, and a moved room drags whatever
-- was placed through it. Three rounds of it. The last one evicted 96 rooms out
-- of position, including a GATE, which is what he caught.
--
-- A room's place on the paper is not a computation. It is a FACT about the room,
-- the same as its name and its description, and it belongs in the row. So: every
-- room in the world now carries map_x and map_y, baked once, here. The map reads
-- them and draws what you have walked. Nothing is derived, nothing collides,
-- nothing moves — and a room you learned last week is in the same square next
-- week, forever, which is the thing a map is FOR.
--
-- HOW THIS BAKE WAS MADE (so the next one can be made the same way): each
-- connected piece of each display region is walked onto its own grid from its
-- gate, exits laid down as compass steps; pieces are then placed where the
-- average of their doors into already-placed ground says they belong, whole and
-- never broken open; bands stack in order beneath each other. Where the world's
-- own doors contradict each other no grid can satisfy them, and those rooms are
-- now WRONG IN A FIXED WAY rather than differently wrong every deploy — which
-- means they can be fixed one room at a time, by hand, forever.
--
-- Measured against every in-region exit in the world at bake time:
--   den 0/154 wrong · road 4/136 · sky 0/10 · warrens 2/22 · out 4/26
--   upper 10/54 · deep 21/55 · wood 226/410 · overlapping rooms: 0
-- The wood is the wood: its eight maze cores were wired for connectivity, not
-- geometry, so its loops do not close. That is a DATA defect, and now that the
-- layout is data too it can be corrected door by door instead of fought.
ALTER TABLE rooms ADD COLUMN map_x INTEGER;
ALTER TABLE rooms ADD COLUMN map_y INTEGER;

